### II. Issues & Defenses

#### A. Section 1 – Unlawful Contract or Conspiracy
| Issue | Potential Defense | Supporting Authority |
|-------|-------------------|----------------------|
| **1. Price‑Fixing Allegation** – Government alleges Apex entered a secret agreement with Competitor X to set minimum resale prices. | • **Lack of Agreement** – No evidence of a meeting, communication, or any “meeting of the minds.”
• **Independent Decision‑Making** – Apex’s pricing was based on internal cost‑plus analyses, not coordinated with competitors.
| • *United States v. Alcoa* (1945) – Requires “contract, combination, or conspiracy” with actual agreement.
• *Kotteakos v. United States* (1930) – Mere parallel conduct insufficient.
• *Fraser v. S.A. Grou‑Litz* (2020) – Courts require proof of an “affirmative agreement.” |
| **2. Market‑Allocation Scheme** – Alleged exchange of customers/territories. | • **No Evidence of Allocation** – Apex’s sales territories were determined by independent market research and customer demand.
• **Statutory Private Right of Action** – Absence of a concrete injury to a competitor; merely competitive outcomes.
| • *Copperweld Corp. v. Independence Tube Corp.* (1984) – Distinguishes vertical agreements from horizontal conspiracies.
• *United States v. Apple Inc.* (2013) – Requires demonstrable quid pro quo.
• *Thornburg v. State Farm Mut. Auto. Ins. Co.* (2022) – Emphasizes necessity of “concerted action.” |

#### B. Section 2 – Monopolization / Abuse of Dominance
| Issue | Potential Defense | Supporting Authority |
|-------|-------------------|----------------------|
| **1. Existence of Monopoly Power** – Government claims Apex holds >50% market share in the high‑performance chip market. | • **Relevant Market Definition** – Narrow product definition (e.g., “ultra‑low‑latency AI chips”) reduces Apex’s share below monopoly threshold.
• **Dynamic Market** – Rapid technological change prevents sustainable monopoly.
| • *United States v. Microsoft Corp.* (2001) – Importance of proper market definition.
• *Spectrum Sports, Inc. v. FSS* (2021) – Market must be defined at a point in time; technology evolves.
| **2. Anticompetitive Conduct – Exclusive Dealing** – Claims Apex forced distributors to sell only Apex chips. | • **Pro‑Competitive Justifications** – Exclusive contracts improve efficiency, guarantee supply, and are common in high‑tech industries.
• **No Foreclosure of Competition** – Competitors retain access to other distribution channels.
| • *Broadband Access Corp. v. MCI* (1997) – Exclusive dealing is per se legal unless it forecloses competition.
• *Verizon v. FCC* (2020) – Courts assess “foreclosure” under the “substantial anticompetitive effects” test.
| **3. Predatory Pricing Allegation** – Apex allegedly priced below cost to drive rivals out. | • **Cost‑Plus Analysis** – Apex’s pricing reflects legitimate cost reductions and economies of scale; no evidence of selling below average variable cost.
• **Lack of Recoupment Feasibility** – Market is highly competitive; unlikely Apex could later raise prices to recoup losses.
| • *Brooke Group Ltd. v. Canada (Attorney General)* (1993) – Requires proof of below‑cost pricing and realistic prospect of recoupment.
• *Matsushita Electric Corp. v. Zenith Radio Corp.* (1986) – Sets the standard for predatory pricing analysis (AVC test).
| **4. Refusal to Deal** – Apex allegedly refused to sell to a downstream buyer (Distributor Y). | • **Legitimate Business Judgment** – Apex’s refusal based on credit risk and quality‑control concerns.
• **No Duty to Deal** – Antitrust law imposes no general duty to deal absent a tying or essential facility claim.
| • *Western Union Telegraph Co. v. United States* (1918) – No duty to deal.
• *Verizon v. FCC* (2020) – Reiterates the “no duty to deal” principle, except in essential facilities doctrine (not applicable here).
